.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, tests (the canary is deliberately untouched)' — 'tests' is generic and does not enumerate the new file."],"finding":"The test file `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` named in AC #1's proving command does not exist in the repo; the plan's 'Files' section lists it only as 'tests' (generic) without explicitly naming this new file as a creation target.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ff154540f92b99629","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria #1 / Files section","norm_id":"n1a4b6b29cfd5253c","priority":0.1886,"reason":"low-validity","scenarios":["An implementer reading the Files section would not know a new test file must be created at tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py.","A reviewer checking 'every file the plan names exists' would correctly flag the proving-command path as absent — it is a new artifact, not an existing one, and the plan doesn't call that out."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Change the Files section to: '`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` (new, unit workflow-lint assertions for AC #1 and AC #2)'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4286,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a positive rationale in the plan explaining why a targeted `git config -C .tickets-tracker merge.ours.driver true` step is preferred over calling `rebar init` in `reconcile-bridge.yml` (e.g., `rebar init` requires Python/rebar already installed, writes `.env-id`, may commit to the tickets branch — all unnecessary side effects for this single driver registration).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan states: 'the canary already gets merge.ours.driver via its existing rebar init call' — implicitly endorsing the canary's approach while choosing a different approach for reconcile-bridge.yml, but never explaining why","reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:146 — `rebar init` call is the alternative mechanism that achieves the same outcome","init.py:499-511 — `_merge_ours_unit` is the unit rebar init calls; it performs only one git config write but is reached only after Python + rebar are installed","A targeted `git config` step can be placed immediately after the worktree add, before Python setup, whereas `rebar init` requires the Python + rebar install steps (lines 129-142) to complete first — an ordering dependency the plan does not surface"],"finding":"The plan does not state a positive rationale explaining why a direct `git config` step was chosen over calling `rebar init` in `reconcile-bridge.yml` (the approach the canary uses to obtain the same driver). Without this, an implementer comparing the two workflows could plausibly follow the canary's pattern and add `rebar init`, which is heavier (creates `.env-id`, may commit to the tickets branch, requires rebar to be installed first) and introduces unnecessary side effects into the reconciler workflow.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc283399973ae78b5","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan § 'Why' / 'Scope correction' / 'Files' — no positive rationale section","norm_id":"n91bfe1e956aec0fc","priority":0.0754,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","scenarios":["Implementer sees the canary uses `rebar init` for the same driver, follows the same pattern in reconcile-bridge.yml, and adds a heavier step that commits to the tickets branch or creates .env-id unexpectedly","Implementer places a `rebar init` call before Python is installed (wrong ordering) because the plan gives no rationale for the targeted approach"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Under the 'Why' or 'Files' section, add: 'A direct `git -C .tickets-tracker config merge.ours.driver true` step is used rather than `rebar init` because: (1) it can be placed immediately after the worktree add, before Python/rebar are installed; (2) it avoids `rebar init`'s side effects (`.env-id` creation, potential `.gitattributes` commits); (3) the reconciler workflow is not a headless-CI provisioning context — it should not run full store initialisation every pass.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2857,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}}],"impact_model_version":"plan-v4","